43 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247143 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186247143 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4fd5592c-2636-5906-98b4-b07ec3e92462" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247143 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(4)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- one $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =3,$ and\n\n- one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =4.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(3) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(4) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =3.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only \n$y$\nis an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-3I)y=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186247156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247156 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247156 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247156 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -19 & -15 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ c & ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{c}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186247156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ad215ed7-9898-586b-9058-ef32fcbf2cd5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247156 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-1)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- a single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =3,$ and\n\n- a single $1\\times 1$ block corresponding to $\\lambda =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(3) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(-1) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =3.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -19 & -15 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -19 & -15 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -19 & -15 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-19){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}-({x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186366979 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366979 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366979 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366979 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366979 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366979 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366979 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366979 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186366983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366979 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c94e7f38-34a4-5dfd-85c2-b0df8007af43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366980 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366980 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] 